{%- comment -%}
  ow-region-selector — the "Site preferences" country/language picker, backed
  by Shopify's real localization form.

  Two INDEPENDENT native <select> fields, not paired rows. An earlier version
  of this snippet cross-joined localization.available_countries with
  localization.available_languages, generating one row per combination
  ('Taiwan — English', 'Taiwan — Chinese', 'Canada — English', ...). That was
  wrong: Liquid has no country.available_languages, so the cross-product could
  not know a language is published for one market and not another, and the
  row count multiplied fast enough to overrun the panel. Two selects avoid
  both problems — each list is exactly what Shopify publishes, with no
  invented pairing between them — matching how Dawn's own footer localization
  form does it (two separate <select> elements, one shared submit).

  Accepts:
  - id_suffix: {String} unique per instance ('header', 'menu') — the selector
    appears more than once per page and form/field ids must not collide.
  - inline: {Boolean} true renders the plain in-page variant used at the
    bottom of the mobile menu — no dialog, no backdrop, no title, no Cancel.
    false renders the desktop trigger button + "Site preferences" dialog.
  - country_filter: {String} optional comma-separated ISO codes ('HK, TW, GB').
    Blank shows every country Markets publishes. See THE FILTER IS A NARROWING
    below — it cannot add a country.

  Usage:
  {% render 'ow-region-selector', id_suffix: 'header', inline: false %}

  THE FILTER IS A NARROWING, NOT A LIST. localization.available_countries comes
  from the store's Markets settings; Liquid cannot invent a country that
  Markets does not publish, and shipping to one it does not cover would fail
  at checkout. So country_filter can only hide options. A code in the filter
  that Markets does not publish is silently ignored, which is the safe
  direction. There is no equivalent filter for languages — available_languages
  is whatever Settings > Languages publishes, in full, every time.

  The shopper's current country always appears as an option, filter or no
  filter — hiding the market they are actually in would leave the selected
  option absent from its own list, which HTML does not allow.

  ONE FORM, ONE SUBMIT. Both selects live inside a single
  {% form 'localization' %} so a shopper picks country AND language, then
  presses Save once — Shopify's form takes country_code and locale_code in the
  same submit, so this is what it actually models. Changing a <select> does
  not submit by itself; assets/ow-chrome.js only wires up the dialog's
  open/close and the desktop Cancel button, everything else is a plain HTML
  form that works with JavaScript disabled.

  DESKTOP IS A <dialog>. Opening it (the trigger button) does need
  JavaScript — .showModal() has no CSS-only equivalent — so with JS disabled
  the desktop trigger button becomes inert. That is a real regression against
  the previous CSS-hover version for the no-JS case; accepted because a
  Cancel/Save modal cannot be built any other way, and the storefront has no
  other JS-free critical path gated on this control.

  MOBILE HAS NO DIALOG. It renders inside the mobile menu takeover
  (#menuModal), which is already the "modal" — wrapping it in a second one
  would nest two dialogs. It is two labelled selects and a Save button, always
  visible once the menu is open, no extra disclosure toggle.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- assign current = localization.country -%}
{%- assign current_lang = localization.language -%}
{%- assign multi_country = false -%}
{%- if localization.available_countries.size > 1 -%}{%- assign multi_country = true -%}{%- endif -%}
{%- assign multi_lang = false -%}
{%- if localization.available_languages.size > 1 -%}{%- assign multi_lang = true -%}{%- endif -%}

{%- if multi_country or multi_lang -%}
  {%- assign allowed = '' -%}
  {%- if country_filter != blank -%}
    {%- assign allowed = country_filter | upcase | replace: ' ', '' | split: ',' -%}
  {%- endif -%}

  {%- assign region_form_id = 'ow-localization-' | append: id_suffix -%}

  <div class="navbar-region{% if inline %} navbar-region--inline{% endif %}">
    {%- unless inline -%}
      <button type="button" class="navbar-lang" data-region-trigger aria-haspopup="dialog" aria-expanded="false" aria-controls="{{ region_form_id }}-dialog">
        <span class="fi fi-{{ current.iso_code | downcase }} navbar-region-flag"></span>
        <span class="navbar-region-label">{{ current.iso_code }} ({{ current.currency.symbol }})</span>
        <img class="navbar-lang-chevron" src="{{ 'ow-chevron-lang.svg' | asset_url }}" alt="" width="16" height="16">
      </button>
    {%- endunless -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      A real <dialog> on desktop (native focus trap, Escape-to-close,
      ::backdrop) with no dialog chrome at all on mobile — same markup, the
      inline modifier just strips the element down to a plain container via
      CSS. Two variants of this file would drift; one that degrades does not.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- comment -%}
      `open` when inline: a <dialog> with no open attribute is display:none by
      the UA stylesheet, engaged only via showModal()/show(). The inline
      variant is never opened by JS — there is no trigger to open it, it is
      meant to sit visibly in the mobile menu's flow — so without `open` here
      it would render nothing at all. Only inline sets it; the desktop dialog
      must start closed for showModal() in ow-chrome.js to have something to
      do, and starting it open would also engage modal/top-layer behaviour
      before any user action.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <dialog class="region-panel" id="{{ region_form_id }}-dialog"{% if inline %} open{% endif %} aria-label="{{ 'localization.site_preferences_title' | t }}">
      {%- unless inline -%}
        <div class="region-panel-head">
          <h3 class="region-panel-title">{{ 'localization.site_preferences_title' | t }}</h3>
          <button type="button" class="region-panel-close" data-region-close aria-label="{{ 'accessibility.close' | t }}">
            <svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true">
              <path d="M18 6L6 18M6 6l12 12" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round"/>
            </svg>
          </button>
        </div>
      {%- endunless -%}

      {%- form 'localization', id: region_form_id, class: 'region-form' -%}
        {%- if multi_country -%}
          <div class="region-field">
            <label for="{{ region_form_id }}-country">{{ 'localization.country_label' | t }}</label>
            <select id="{{ region_form_id }}-country" name="country_code">
              {%- for country in localization.available_countries -%}
                {%- comment -%}
                  `unless` rather than `if ... contains x == false` — Liquid has
                  no operator precedence, it evaluates right to left and does
                  not group, so a comparison chained onto `contains` inside an
                  `and` does not mean what it reads like.
                {%- endcomment -%}
                {%- if allowed != '' -%}
                  {%- if country.iso_code != current.iso_code -%}
                    {%- unless allowed contains country.iso_code -%}{%- continue -%}{%- endunless -%}
                  {%- endif -%}
                {%- endif -%}
                <option value="{{ country.iso_code }}"{% if country.iso_code == current.iso_code %} selected{% endif %}>{{ country.name }} ({{ country.currency.iso_code }} {{ country.currency.symbol }})</option>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </select>
          </div>
        {%- else -%}
          <input type="hidden" name="country_code" value="{{ current.iso_code }}">
        {%- endif -%}

        {%- if multi_lang -%}
          <div class="region-field">
            <label for="{{ region_form_id }}-lang">{{ 'localization.language_label' | t }}</label>
            <select id="{{ region_form_id }}-lang" name="locale_code">
              {%- for language in localization.available_languages -%}
                <option value="{{ language.iso_code }}"{% if language.iso_code == current_lang.iso_code %} selected{% endif %}>{{ language.endonym_name | capitalize }}</option>
              {%- endfor -%}
            </select>
          </div>
        {%- else -%}
          <input type="hidden" name="locale_code" value="{{ current_lang.iso_code }}">
        {%- endif -%}

        <div class="region-panel-actions">
          {%- unless inline -%}
            <button type="button" class="btn-outline region-cancel" data-region-close>{{ 'localization.cancel' | t }}</button>
          {%- endunless -%}
          <button type="submit" class="btn-primary region-save">{{ 'localization.save' | t }}</button>
        </div>
      {%- endform -%}
    </dialog>
  </div>
{%- endif -%}
